<!-- ARTICLE -->
<article class="post-body">

<!-- §1 The Puzzle Problem -->
<section id="s-puzzle" data-code="puzzle">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Puzzle Problem</div>
<h2>Why do big LLMs struggle at Sudoku?</h2>
<p>
  Solving a 9×9 Sudoku requires placing digits 1–9 such that every row, column, and box contains each digit exactly once. There is exactly one correct answer. GPT-5, claude-opus-4, and gemini-3 all struggle: the best single LLM (claude-opus-4) scores only 34.7% on the PPBench golden set. Why?
</p>
<p>
  Autoregressive LLMs generate answers <em>token by token, left to right</em>. Constraint satisfaction requires holding and revising a global solution state across all 81 cells simultaneously — something the one-shot token generation window is not designed for. Chain-of-thought helps a little, but the fundamental issue remains.
</p>
<div class="callout callout-insight">
  <span class="callout-icon">💡</span>
  <span><strong>The key insight:</strong> Some reasoning tasks are not about generating text — they are about <em>converging</em> to a consistent global state. Iterative refinement of a hidden state is a fundamentally better fit than token-by-token generation.</span>
</div>
<p>
  Tiny Recursive Models (TRM) take this idea seriously. At just 7M parameters they outperform frontier LLMs on reasoning puzzles. But they have a silent weakness: they can get <em>stuck</em>. PTRM fixes that with a two-line change at inference time.
</p>
</section>

<!-- §2 TRM Architecture -->
<section id="s-trm" data-code="trm">
<div class="section-no">02 — TRM Architecture</div>
<h2>Recursive refinement: how TRM thinks</h2>
<p>
  TRM maintains two state vectors — a <strong>latent</strong> <code>z</code> (hidden reasoning state) and an <strong>answer</strong> <code>y</code> — and iteratively refines them using a single two-layer network <code>fθ</code>. The entire "chain of thought" happens silently inside these vectors.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">trm-recursion.diagram</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">step through the loop</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="trm-stepper" id="trm-stepper">
      <div class="trm-step-box" id="trm-s0">
        <div class="trm-step-icon">📥</div>
        <div class="trm-step-title">Initialize</div>
        <div class="trm-step-code">z₀ = 0<br>y₀ = 0</div>
      </div>
      <div class="trm-step-box" id="trm-s1">
        <div class="trm-step-icon">🔄</div>
        <div class="trm-step-title">Latent update (×n)</div>
        <div class="trm-step-code">z ← fθ(x + y + z)</div>
      </div>
      <div class="trm-step-box" id="trm-s2">
        <div class="trm-step-icon">✏️</div>
        <div class="trm-step-title">Answer update (×1)</div>
        <div class="trm-step-code">y ← fθ(y + z)</div>
      </div>
      <div class="trm-step-box" id="trm-s3">
        <div class="trm-step-icon">🔁</div>
        <div class="trm-step-title">Deep recursion</div>
        <div class="trm-step-code">repeat T times</div>
      </div>
      <div class="trm-step-box" id="trm-s4">
        <div class="trm-step-icon">🎯</div>
        <div class="trm-step-title">Decode answer</div>
        <div class="trm-step-code">ŷ = argmax fO(y)</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="trm-nav">
      <button id="trm-prev" disabled>‹ Prev</button>
      <button id="trm-next">Next ›</button>
      <span class="trm-prog" id="trm-prog">Step 1 of 5</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>One network does everything</h3>
<p>
  There is <strong>one shared network <code>fθ</code></strong> for all three operations. It distinguishes "latent update" from "answer update" by whether the input includes <code>x</code> (the puzzle). This is extremely parameter-efficient: 7M parameters is roughly the size of a single attention head in GPT-4.
</p>
<p>
  A "deep recursion" chains T of these (latent-update × n → answer-update) sequences. At inference you can run more deep recursions than during training — this is <em>depth scaling</em>, giving TRM extra compute without retraining.
</p>

<h3>The Q head: TRM's self-grader</h3>
<p>
  A linear <strong>Q head</strong> <code>fQ(y)</code> outputs a scalar score predicting whether the current answer is correct. It's trained jointly via binary cross-entropy against the ground-truth correctness label. During training it enables adaptive early stopping; at inference PTRM repurposes it as a <em>verifier</em>.
</p>
</section>

<!-- §3 Latent Basins -->
<section id="s-basins" data-code="basins">
<div class="section-no">03 — Latent Basins</div>
<h2>Why TRM gets stuck: the basin problem</h2>
<p>
  Think of the latent space as a landscape with valleys. Each valley is a <strong>basin</strong> — a region the trajectory settles into. A <strong>good basin</strong> decodes to the correct answer. A <strong>bad basin</strong> decodes to a wrong one and has no way out: the deterministic recursion just keeps cycling inside it.
</p>
<p>
  The paper identifies three trajectory modes by projecting latent states through PCA:
</p>

<div class="mode-grid">
  <div class="mode-card mode-success">
    <div class="mode-card-title">Quick Success</div>
    <p>Trajectory moves directly to a good basin in a few steps. Q value and accuracy both rise fast and stay high.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="mode-card mode-delayed">
    <div class="mode-card-title">Delayed Success</div>
    <p>Trajectory oscillates in a bad basin for a while, then suddenly escapes to a good basin. Q spikes with it.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="mode-card mode-fail">
    <div class="mode-card-title">Failure</div>
    <p>Trajectory oscillates in a bad basin forever. Accuracy never approaches 100%; Q stays negative throughout.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">latent-basin.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click a mode below</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="basin-canvas"></canvas>
    <div class="basin-controls">
      <button class="basin-btn active" id="basin-success">Quick Success</button>
      <button class="basin-btn" id="basin-delayed">Delayed Success</button>
      <button class="basin-btn" id="basin-fail">Failure</button>
      <span class="basin-mode-label" id="basin-label" style="margin-left:auto;font-size:.65rem;opacity:.7">trajectory shown in 2D PCA projection</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>The Q head tracks quality perfectly</h3>
<p>
  Across 100 validation puzzles, the paper shows the Q logit sharply separates correct trajectories (converges to +6) from incorrect ones (stays at −6). This means TRM already <em>knows</em> whether a trajectory is good — it just can't generate a different one when stuck. PTRM gives it that ability.
</p>

<div class="callout callout-tip">
  <span class="callout-icon">⚡</span>
  <span>The fix is conceptually simple: if the model can tell good from bad trajectories, just run <em>multiple</em> trajectories and pick the best one. The only missing ingredient is stochasticity — which Gaussian noise provides for free.</span>
</div>
</section>

<!-- §4 PTRM -->
<section id="s-ptrm" data-code="ptrm">
<div class="section-no">04 — PTRM: Escape with Noise</div>
<h2>Two-line inference fix that changes everything</h2>
<p>
  PTRM adds a single modification to TRM inference: at each deep recursion step, inject Gaussian noise into the latent before the forward pass. Then run K such rollouts in parallel and return the answer from the rollout with the highest Q score.
</p>

<div class="callout callout-insight" style="font-family:var(--ff-mono);font-size:.82rem;line-height:2">
  <span class="callout-icon" style="font-family:sans-serif">📐</span>
  <span>
    <span style="color:var(--muted)">for each step t:</span><br>
    &nbsp;&nbsp;<strong style="color:var(--cyan)">z ← z + ε,  ε ~ N(0, σ²I)</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.72rem">← the only change</span><br>
    &nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--muted2)">z, y ← fθ(x, z, y)</span><br>
    <span style="color:var(--muted)">select rollout k* = argmax_k  fQ(y_k^final)</span>
  </span>
</div>

<p>No retraining. No task-specific augmentation. No verifier. Just noise and the Q head the model already has.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">ptrm-rollouts.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">adjust K below</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="rollout-canvas"></canvas>
    <div class="rollout-controls">
      <button class="basin-btn" id="roll-k1">K=1</button>
      <button class="basin-btn active" id="roll-k5">K=5</button>
      <button class="basin-btn" id="roll-k25">K=25</button>
      <button class="basin-btn" id="roll-k100">K=100</button>
      <span class="k-label" style="margin-left:auto">rollouts: <span id="k-val">5</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp; green = escaped, red = stuck</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>Why does noise help?</h3>
<p>
  Even a small perturbation to <code>z</code> can push the trajectory out of a bad basin. Among K=100 rollouts of a stuck puzzle, the paper observes that ~8% escape to a good basin. At K=1 and K=5 no rollout escapes; at K=25 one does; at K=100 eight do. The escape probability compounds with K — which is the core of width scaling.
</p>
<p>
  Note that the good-basin rollouts are a <em>minority</em>. The Q head is what makes this work: it reliably scores them higher than the stuck majority, so you don't need a separate verifier.
</p>
</section>

<!-- §5 Scaling -->
<section id="s-scaling" data-code="scaling">
<div class="section-no">05 — Width vs Depth Scaling</div>
<h2>Two knobs: K rollouts vs D recursion steps</h2>
<p>
  TRM already supports <strong>depth scaling</strong>: running more deep recursion steps at inference than during training. This is sequential — you can't parallelize it. PTRM adds <strong>width scaling</strong>: K independent rollouts that run in parallel.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">width-scaling.canvas</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="scale-canvas"></canvas>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:1.25rem;margin-top:.75rem;flex-wrap:wrap">
      <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.4rem;font-size:.71rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)"><div style="width:12px;height:3px;background:var(--purple);border-radius:2px"></div>pass@K (oracle)</div>
      <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.4rem;font-size:.71rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)"><div style="width:12px;height:3px;background:var(--cyan);border-radius:2px"></div>best-Q@K (Q head picks)</div>
      <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.4rem;font-size:.71rem;font-family:var(--muted);color:var(--muted)"><div style="width:12px;height:3px;background:rgba(92,99,112,.6);border-radius:2px"></div>mode@K (majority vote)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>Width wins, depth is a consolation prize</h3>
<p>
  Tripling depth from 16 → 48 steps gains only +3.1pp at K=1. Scaling K from 1 → 100 gains +13pp. And the two are largely additive: combining K=100 with D=48 gives the best overall result (+28.6pp over the K=1, D=16 baseline). Rollouts are also fully parallelizable on GPU — depth adds serial latency, width does not (per rollout).
</p>
<p>
  The <strong>gap between pass@K and best-Q@K is under 1pp</strong> on PPBench and Sudoku-Extreme — meaning the Q head is essentially a perfect verifier on these tasks. On Maze-Hard it's weaker, leaving headroom for future work.
</p>

<table class="cost-table">
  <thead><tr><th>Config</th><th>PPBench Agg.</th><th>Depth</th><th>Width</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td class="mn">TRM K=1, D=16</td><td>62.6%</td><td>baseline</td><td>1 rollout</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="mn">TRM K=1, D=48</td><td>66.0%</td><td>+3×</td><td>1 rollout</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="mn">PTRM K=100, D=16</td><td>89.8%</td><td>baseline</td><td>100 rollouts</td></tr>
    <tr class="ptrm-row"><td class="mn">PTRM K=100, D=48</td><td class="best">91.2%</td><td>+3×</td><td>100 rollouts</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</section>

